Z||000001||FieroJoe||06-07-2001||07:40 AM||||Go down to your local Ace Hardware store and look at the ad, I think I remember pricing the same dremel tool set that I have at home, it think maybe 39.99 or something.  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000002||DecadenceR||06-07-2001||08:59 AM||||All depends on what set you get. they have anything from just the dremel to a 500 pc kit.<BR>i think I got mine from Home Depot on sale for $79||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||06-07-2001||09:32 AM||||The set that's on sale at ace is a 38 peice set.<P>BTW, don't invest too much money in a boatload of dremel brand bits.  They really suck.  They are very expensive at retail cost (I get them at wholesale), and wont last you too long.  Don't get the paper type cutting discs, they'll break right apart.  There are stiffer type ones that will fit the mandrel for cutting available.<P>For an assorment of tools, we bought a 30 peice set that has a good assortment of shapes and sizes for grinding and cutting from Northern Hydraulics (mail order catalog).  They have held up much better than the dremel brand bits.<P>You might also want to invest in a few Roto-zip bits, they'll work great.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||06-07-2001||09:33 AM||||Depends on how much you are going to do with your Dremel. I used them quite a bit in my old gunstock making days and if you plan on doing a lot of work with them get the ball bearing variable speed unit. It does cost a touch more but much more versitile and you won't wear out the bushings. Again it's only worth the extra expense if you plan on heavy usage.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fiero5||06-07-2001||01:25 PM||||Wal*Mart just started carrying them.<BR>Best prices I have seen, plus they price match.<P>Steve||||reg||12
Z||000006||theogre||06-07-2001||09:00 PM||||Wall mart has allot of dremel stuff. Home Depot and Lowes both do as well. They cost more but they usually have nearly every accessory Dremel makes. Sears has a bunch of stuff as well.||||reg||1
